<blockquote>Former Google CEO Eric Schmidt said in a private lecture in 2025: &quot;In the next decade, the most valuable human skill will not be what you can do, but what you decide AI should do.&quot;</blockquote>

<h2>1. When the &quot;All-Rounder&quot; Arrives</h2>

<p>In January 2026, OpenAI's o3 model scored 87.5% on the ARC-AGI test, approaching human average levels. That same month, DeepSeek V4 was released as open source, matching GPT-4o across multiple benchmarks.</p>

<p>Six months later, both news items have gone from &quot;shocking the tech world&quot; to &quot;oh, we've heard that.&quot;</p>

<p>But a deeper question remains unanswered: <strong>If AI can do everything, what's left for humans?</strong></p>

<h2>2. Three Things AI Can't Do</h2>

<p>Don't panic just yet. Let's break down AI's capabilities and limitations.</p>

<p><strong>What can AI do?</strong> Summarize, create, code, analyze data, generate images, reason... nearly all &quot;cognitive skills&quot; that humans have mastered.</p>

<p><strong>What can't AI do?</strong> The following three things — none of today's AI models can truly do them.</p>

<h3>1. AI Can't &quot;Truly Question&quot;</h3>

<p>AI can be asked to &quot;offer critical opinions,&quot; but its criticism is based on patterns in training data. It doesn't question a conclusion out of genuine unease.</p>

<p>Human questioning often comes from:</p>
<li>Intuitive dissonance (&quot;This feels wrong, but I can't say why&quot;)</li>
<li>Experiential conflict (&quot;I've seen this situation before, and it turned out completely differently&quot;)</li>
<li>Value discomfort (&quot;Logically it's fine, but it just doesn't feel like the right approach&quot;)</li>

<p>These three types of &quot;dissonance&quot; have no equivalent in AI's training paradigm. <strong>AI always optimizes for a &quot;correct output,&quot; but human progress often comes from dissatisfaction with &quot;solutions that are too correct.&quot;</strong></p>

<h3>2. AI Can't &quot;Bear Consequences&quot;</h3>

<p>Deloitte's 2025 <em>State of AI in the Enterprise</em> report found that when deploying AI, the biggest obstacle for companies isn't technical — it's &quot;accountability.&quot; When an AI's advice leads to an error, who takes responsibility?</p>

<p>Humans are trustworthy (at least in some situations) because humans:</p>
<li>Bear the consequences of their own decisions</li>
<li>Hesitate because you might suffer from their actions</li>
<li>Re-examine a plan because you say &quot;I'm not comfortable with this approach&quot;</li>

<p>AI doesn't. AI has no &quot;conscience&quot; state.</p>

<h3>3. AI Can't &quot;Create Meaning&quot;</h3>

<p>This is the most fundamental one.</p>

<p>AI can write a poem about &quot;loneliness,&quot; but fundamentally, it doesn't know what loneliness feels like. AI can perfectly mimic an empathetic conversation, but it has no inner state of &quot;I truly care about you.&quot;</p>

<p>All human effort and creation ultimately points to one question: <strong>What is the meaning of this?</strong></p>

<p>AI can't answer that. Not because the technology isn't advanced enough, but because &quot;meaning&quot; isn't something that can be trained with data.</p>

<h2>3. MIT's Research Provides the Answer</h2>

<p>MIT Sloan's Paul Daugherty and H James Wilson proposed a framework in their 2025 research: <strong>&quot;Human Advantage&quot; = Judgment × Relational Skill × Curiosity</strong></p>

<p>Tracking AI deployment across over 300 companies, they found:</p>

<li><strong>Companies pursuing only automation</strong> (using AI to replace human work) saw short-term efficiency gains, but competitiveness generally declined after 12 months — because automation eliminated learning opportunities, causing team capabilities to regress.</li>
<li><strong>Companies pursuing augmentation</strong> (using AI to amplify human capabilities) showed clear long-term compound growth advantages — every $1 invested in AI produced $3.20 in returns within 18 months.</li>

<p>This finding aligns with HBR research published in late 2025: <strong>&quot;Augmentation beats automation — not as a value choice, but as an economic fact.&quot;</strong></p>

<h2>4. So What Is Human Core Competence, Really?</h2>

<p>With all that said, here's a clear framework.</p>

<p>In the AI era, human core competence is not &quot;doing better than AI,&quot; but <strong>&quot;doing what AI can't.&quot;</strong> Specifically, it's a combination of four capabilities:</p>

<table>
  <tr><td>Capability</td><td>Description</td><td>How to Cultivate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>🔍 **Judgment**</td><td>Making decisions with incomplete information; quality-checking AI outputs</td><td>Engage with ambiguous problems, make trade-off decisions</td></tr>
  <tr><td>🤝 **Relational Skill**</td><td>Building trust, collaboration, and connection</td><td>Face-to-face communication, active listening, real teamwork</td></tr>
  <tr><td>💡 **Curiosity**</td><td>Asking &quot;why,&quot; &quot;what if,&quot; and &quot;is there a better way&quot;</td><td>Broad reading, cross-disciplinary exchange, keep &quot;useless hobbies&quot;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>🎯 **Meaning-making**</td><td>Knowing what matters and what's worth doing</td><td>Self-reflection, values clarification, goal alignment</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Not one of these four capabilities is a &quot;technical skill.&quot; They won't become obsolete, they can't be replaced by AI, but they also can't be acquired quickly through &quot;cramming&quot; or &quot;online courses.&quot;</p>

<p>They require real practice, real trial-and-error, and real human interaction.</p>

<h2>5. A Question More Worth Thinking About Than &quot;Being Replaced&quot;</h2>

<p>Every time I write this kind of article, it feels like I should end with reassurance: &quot;Don't worry, AI won't replace you.&quot;</p>

<p>But I think the question truly worth asking is not <strong>&quot;Will AI replace you?&quot;</strong> but:</p>

<p><strong>If AI makes &quot;being able to do it&quot; completely worthless, what about you is worth remembering?</strong></p>

<p>People who have figured this out don't need anyone to tell them what their &quot;core competence&quot; is.</p>

<p>Because they already have it.</p>

